[[en:Kōshin]]
{{Sexagenary cycle comprehensive}}
'''Yang Metal Monkey also known as <!--please transliterate all the hiragana readings into romaji and provide the kanji--> Gengshen''' (庚申; かのえさる、こうきんのさる、こうしん) is one of the {{ill|Sexagenary cycle|ja|干支}}.
[[fa:کوشین شینکو]][[zh:庚申信仰]][[ja:庚申信仰]][[ru:Косин]][[fr:Kōshin]]
It is the 57th combination in the Sexagenary cycle comprehensive, preceded by {{ill|Kimi|ja|己未}} and followed by {{ill|Shinyuu|ja|辛酉}}. In {{ill|Yin-Yang Five Elements|ja|陰陽五行}}, the stem {{ill|Geng|ja|庚}} of the {{ill|Ten Heavenly Stems|ja|十干}} represents Yang Metal, and the branch {{ill|Shen|ja|申}} of the {{ill|Twelve Earthly Branches|ja|十二支}} also represents Yang Metal, a state referred to as {{ill|Bihwa|ja|五行思想#比和（ひわ）|lt=Bihwa|lt_ja=比和}}.

== Gengshen Years ==
{{ill|Western Calendar|ja|西暦}} and {{ill|Imperial Era|ja|神武天皇即位紀元|lt=Imperial Era|lt_ja=皇紀}} designate years that are multiples of 60 as {{ill|Year|ja|年}} of Gengshen.

=== Nature and Era Change ===
Because both the heavenly stem and earthly branch of Gengshen are of {{ill|Metal|ja|金属|lt=Metal|lt_ja=金}} nature, it was believed that the metal energy pervading the universe in a Gengshen year or day would render human hearts cold and unfeeling. The following combination, {{ill|Shinyuu|ja|辛酉}}, also being metal in nature—with Xin being of Yin energy—was thought to intensify this coldness. Consequently, Gengshen and Shinyuu were considered harbingers of political upheaval, and to avert such changes it was not uncommon to change eras for two consecutive years. For example, this occurred in the first years of {{ill|Man'en|ja|万延}} ({{ill|1860|ja|1860年}}) and {{ill|Bunkyu|ja|文久}} ({{ill|1861|ja|1861年}}).

